    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-279719",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse",
      "problem": "Determine the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x-4y=-20.$\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x-4y & =-20 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+12x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}+12x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-20 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+6x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-20 \\\\ 2[(x+3)^{2}-9]+[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =-20 \\\\ 2(x+3)^{2}-18+(y-2)^{2}-4 & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+3)^{2}-18+(y-2)^{2}-4 & =-20 \\\\ 2(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =-20+18+4 \\\\ 2(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{1}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sqrt{2}>1,$ the length of the major axis  $=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2},$ and the length of the minor axis  $=2\\cdot 1=2.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
