      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse",
      "problem": "Consider the ellipse whose equation is $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}–48x+72y+144=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Its center is $(6,-4)$\n- The length of the major axis is $6$\n- The length of the minor axis is $4$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}–48x+72y+144 & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-12x]+9[{y}^{2}+8y]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}]+9[(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-36]+9[(y+4)^{2}-16]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =-144+144+144 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6>4,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 6=12,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(6,-4).$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, statements II and III are false.",
