      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse",
      "problem": "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $25x^2 + 9y^2 - 100x - 18y = 116.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-100x-18y & =116 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}-4x]+9[{y}^{2}-2y] & =116 \\\\ 25[(x-2)^{2}-4]+9[(y-1)^{2}-1] & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =116+100+9 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =225 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5 > 3,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 5 =10,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 3 =6,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({2},{1}).$",
