      "problem": "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $x^2 + 4y^2 - 6x + 24y + 41 = 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-6x+24y+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+4[{y}^{2}+6y]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4[(y+3)^{2}-9]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =9+36-41 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2 =4,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 1 =2,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({3},{-3}).$",
